        "math_academy_id": "q-4555",
        "knowledge_point_id": "d0703e1b-2b81-5b95-b2ad-a7e42e702c21",
        "knowledge_point": "Applying the Second Fundamental Theorem of Calculus Using the Chain Rule",
        "problem": "For $x>2$, find ${f}^{′}(x)$ if $f(x)={\\int }_{2}^{\\sqrt{x}}\\frac{1}{2-{t}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t.$",
        "worked_solution": "To compute ${f}^{′}(x),$ we first make the substitution $u=\\sqrt{x}$ and then we apply the chain rule, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}{\\int }_{2}^{\\sqrt{x}}\\frac{1}{2-{t}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}u}{\\int }_{2}^{u}\\frac{1}{2-{t}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe integrand is continuous on $[2,u],$ and so by FTC II we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2-{u}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\sqrt{x})\\cdot \\frac{1}{2-{u}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{x}}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2-(\\sqrt{x})^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{x}(2-x)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
        "answer_fields": [
          {
