        "problem": "A function $p(t)$ has values according to the table below:\n\n| $t$ | $-2$ | $-1$ | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $p(t)$ | $3$ | $6$ | $9$ | $5$ | $1$ | $0$ |\n\nApproximate ${p}^{′}(t)$ at the point $t=0$ using a forward difference approximation.",
        "worked_solution": "We use the forward difference approximation\n\n$$\n{p}^{′}(t)≈\\frac{p(t+h)-p(t)}{h}\n$$\n\nat the point $t=0.$ We choose the step size $h$ to be as small as possible, in this case $h=1.$ This gives \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{p}^{′}(0) & ≈\\frac{p(0+1)-p(0)}{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{p(1)-p(0)}{1} \\\\  & =5-9 \\\\  & =-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
