        "math_academy_id": "q-47378",
        "knowledge_point_id": "5e651cb4-ae24-5ac7-9c01-d90de562b155",
        "knowledge_point": "Estimating a Derivative From a Table Using a Forward Difference Approximation: Word Problem",
        "problem": "Andrey wants to estimate his running speed. Andrey's friend Max takes note of Andrey's position while he is on the track. Max's observations are given in the table below, where $s(t)$ is the distance covered, measured in meters, $t$ seconds after Andrey started running.\n\n| $t$ | $0$ | $2$ | $4$ | $6$ | $8$ | $10$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $s(t)$ | $0$ | $4$ | $9$ | $15$ | $20$ | $27$ |\n\nApproximate Andrey's speed at the point where $t=6$ seconds using a forward difference approximation.",
        "worked_solution": "We use the forward difference approximation\n\n$$\n{s}^{′}(t)≈\\frac{s(t+h)-s(t)}{h}\n$$\n\n \nat the point $t=6.$ We choose the step size $h$ to be as small as possible, in this case $h=2.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{s}^{′}(6) & ≈\\frac{s(6+2)-s(6)}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{s(8)-s(6)}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{20-15}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{2} \\\\  & =2.5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo at $t=6$ Andrey's speed is approximately $2.5\\,\\text{m/s}.$",
